<PaperLayout
  title="Policy-Aligned Predictive Verification"
  subtitle="for Adaptive VLA Action-Chunk Execution"
  venue="Preprint · 2026"
  authors={authors}
  links={links}
  description="PAV decides how much of a VLA's action chunk to execute by predicting each prefix's consequence in the policy's own representation space and verifying that successful continuation remains possible."
>
  <section>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/pav/img/teaser.png"
        alt="Generation stability and prediction agreement stay high through a missed grasp and task failure, while proposal validity drops and triggers earlier replanning that leads to success"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Generation stability (a) and prediction agreement (b) can remain high despite a
        missed grasp and task failure. Proposal validity (c) guides earlier replanning,
        leading to success in this example.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Abstract</h2>
    <p>
      Vision-language-action (VLA) policies generate action sequences that reduce repeated
      inference, but deciding how much of each sequence to execute is critical to task
      success. Existing adaptive methods make this decision using generation stability or
      prediction agreement, yet these signals do not establish whether executing a
      candidate prefix preserves the ability to complete the task. We introduce a
      <strong>policy-aligned predictive verifier (PAV)</strong> that anticipates the
      consequences of candidate action prefixes and evaluates their compatibility with
      successful continuation before execution. Rather than using future prediction to
      generate actions, our method evaluates existing proposals in the VLA's internal
      task-conditioned representation space. To learn local judgments from terminal task
      outcomes, we combine trajectory-level survival supervision with a localization prior
      that preserves useful behavior within failed rollouts. The resulting validity scores
      guide execution-horizon selection. Evaluations with π<sub>0</sub> and
      π<sub>0.5</sub> across LIBERO, RoboTwin 2.0, and real-world manipulation show
      improved success–query trade-offs. These findings point toward a different role for
      future prediction in robotics, using it to assess how far to trust a policy's
      proposed actions rather than to generate new ones.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Key numbers</h2>
    <div class="stats">
      <div class="stat">
        <div class="stat-num">+7.5 pp</div>
        <div class="stat-label">LIBERO success vs. h = 5 (π<sub>0.5</sub>, H = 50)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <div class="stat-num">−63%</div>
        <div class="stat-label">VLA queries in the same setting</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <div class="stat-num">74.4%</div>
        <div class="stat-label">Real-world success (best fixed: 41.1%)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <div class="stat-num">≤ 1.24%</div>
        <div class="stat-label">Verifier compute vs. action generation</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>1. The problem: how long to trust an action chunk</h2>
    <p>
      VLA policies such as π<sub>0</sub> output a chunk of <em>H</em> future actions per
      query. Executing the whole chunk saves inference but delays feedback, so systems
      usually execute only a prefix of length <em>h</em> and replan. Picking <em>h</em>
      well is the crux: too short wastes queries, too long lets errors accumulate.
    </p>
    <p>
      Existing adaptive methods pick <em>h</em> from <strong>generation stability</strong>
      (do the actions regenerate consistently?) or <strong>prediction agreement</strong>
      (do predicted and observed dynamics match?). Neither establishes whether executing a
      prefix still leaves the task completable — a proposal can be consistently generated
      and accurately predicted, yet still miss the grasp.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>2. Proposal validity, predicted and verified</h2>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/pav/img/method.png"
        alt="PAV pipeline: the VLA representation and proposed action chunk feed a future-representation predictor; a validity verifier scores each step and the accumulated prefix score selects how many actions to execute"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>
        The predictor uses the VLA representation and proposed actions to predict future
        policy representations. The verifier estimates local validity, which is accumulated
        and normalized to select an execution horizon. The robot executes the selected
        prefix and replans.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      PAV targets <strong>proposal validity</strong> <em>V<sub>t</sub></em>(<em>h</em>): the
      probability that successful continuation remains possible throughout the first
      <em>h</em> actions. Because a compatible prefix has compatible predecessors, validity
      factorizes step by step, and each step splits into prediction and verification.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Predict in policy space.</strong> A predictor maps the VLA's
        task-conditioned representation and an action prefix to the representation the
        policy would see after executing it, trained with a cosine alignment loss plus an
        action-swap margin so that predictions depend on the actions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Verify from task outcomes.</strong> A verifier scores local validity for
        each predicted step; the running product estimates <em>V<sub>t</sub></em>(<em>h</em>).
        Successful rollouts raise every factor, failed rollouts lower only the product, and
        a localization prior concentrates the blame on few steps so that valid behavior
        inside failed rollouts is not penalized.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Select the horizon.</strong> At inference, candidate prefixes are checked
        in ascending order and execution stops at the first length-normalized score below a
        threshold.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>3. Results</h2>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/pav/img/settings.png"
        alt="Real-world tasks Press Button, Stack Cubes and Place Cube in Tray, and simulation environments from LIBERO and RoboTwin 2.0"
        loading="lazy"
      />
