    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>LIBERO.</strong> PAV has the highest average success in all three policy–chunk
      settings. The clearest gain is with π<sub>0.5</sub> at H = 50: +7.50 pp over
      h = 5 with 63.3% fewer queries.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>RoboTwin 2.0.</strong> With π<sub>0.5</sub> (H = 32), PAV reaches 54.13% on
      DVAC's Selected 16 tasks, above the best fixed horizon (47.88%) and DVAC (41.6%), and
      58.64% on all 50 tasks versus 55.76% for the best fixed horizon at similar query
      counts.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="results">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Real world (π<sub>0.5</sub>)</th>
            <th>Place Cube</th>
            <th>Stack</th>
            <th>Press</th>
            <th>Avg.</th>
            <th>Replans ↓</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="method">h = 5</td><td>16.7</td><td>10.0</td><td>23.3</td><td>16.7</td><td>69.58</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="method">h = 10</td><td>66.7</td><td>16.7</td><td>40.0</td><td>41.1</td><td>36.54</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="method">h = 30</td><td>23.3</td><td>43.3</td><td>53.3</td><td>40.0</td><td>11.97</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="method">h = 50</td><td>26.7</td><td>33.3</td><td>50.0</td><td>36.7</td><td>6.91</td></tr>
          <tr class="highlight"><td class="method">PAV</td><td>80.0</td><td>66.7</td><td>76.7</td><td>74.4</td><td>12.87</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="table-caption">
      Success (%) over 30 trials per task on a 7-DoF Franka Panda, with mean replanning
      counts per episode.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Real world.</strong> PAV is best on all three tasks, averaging 74.4% versus
      41.1% for the strongest fixed horizon. At a replanning count similar to h = 30
      (12.87 vs. 11.97), varying the execution length lifts success from 40.0% to 74.4%.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>4. What makes it work</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="results">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Variant (LIBERO, π<sub>0.5</sub>, H = 50)</th>
            <th>Avg. success</th>
            <th>Queries ↓</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="method">Direct (no future prediction)</td><td>84.55</td><td>22.3</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="method">Prediction in DINOv2 space</td><td>87.00</td><td>22.2</td></tr>
          <tr class="highlight"><td class="method">PAV (prediction in policy space)</td><td>94.65</td><td>14.0</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      <strong>Predicting the future matters.</strong> Scoring prefixes directly from current
      representations, with everything else fixed, loses 10.1 pp and needs more queries.
      The predictor is genuinely action-sensitive: on held-out rollouts its prediction
      reaches cosine 0.9401 to the observed future with the executed actions, versus 0.5304
      with same-task swapped actions.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>So does the space it predicts in.</strong> Predicting generic DINOv2 features
      instead of the VLA's task-conditioned representation drops success to 87.00%.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>It is cheap.</strong> Verifier GPU compute takes 0.90–2.49 ms per query, or
      0.45–1.24% of action-generation time across the four settings measured.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Takeaway</h2>
    <p>
      Effective action-chunk execution depends not only on generating coherent actions but
      on deciding how long to commit to them. PAV uses future prediction to assess how far
      to trust a policy's proposal — in the same representation space that generated it —
      rather than to generate new actions.
    </p>
  </section>

